    {
      "id": "GUN-01",
      "chapter": "global",
      "anchor": "global-near-integral-traces",
      "title": "Near rational terms have exactly the first n power traces of the identity",
      "statement": "For a rational term of nonzero coefficient meeting the archimedean singular-value test, the power traces tr(G^j) are exactly n for 1≤j≤n. Finite support and radicality first make every difference tr(G^j)−n divisible by the level, and the archimedean bounds then force it to vanish.",
      "hypotheses": "CM extension E/F, f=[F:ℚ], n≥1, positive integer g₀>4n generating a radical ideal in 𝒪_E, and ε=c/(n⁸f²) for 0<c≤10⁻⁵. At x the conjugated finite term stabilizes the finite lattice and is unipotent modulo g₀ with exponent at most n. The Euclidean norm of its archimedean logarithmic singular values is at most ε at every real place.",
      "dependencies": [],
      "equations": [
        "global-near-lattice-congruence",
        "global-near-divisible-traces"
      ],
